1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:08,305
Can you show me undo working directory changes?

2
00:00:08,867 --> 00:00:12,193
How to throw away edits you have not staged yet:

3
00:00:12,193 --> 00:00:13,371
git restore FILE.

4
00:00:13,371 --> 00:00:18,221
And why this is one of the few Git commands that really destroys work.

5
00:00:19,967 --> 00:00:24,366
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

6
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:27,296
An experiment that went wrong:

7
00:00:27,296 --> 00:00:30,570
Throw away the change to prices.txt only:

8
00:00:31,133 --> 00:00:34,281
Throw away the change to prices.txt only:

9
00:00:34,281 --> 00:00:39,885
prices.txt is back to the committed version; menu.txt still has its edit.

10
00:00:39,885 --> 00:00:43,033
Restore everything in the current folder:

11
00:00:43,567 --> 00:00:49,169
prices.txt is back to the committed version; menu.txt still has its edit.

12
00:00:49,169 --> 00:00:52,316
Restore everything in the current folder:

13
00:00:54,067 --> 00:00:55,889
Let me try something.

14
00:00:55,889 --> 00:00:57,884
Restore the wrong file:

15
00:00:57,884 --> 00:01:00,921
a whole morning's work in menu.txt.

16
00:01:01,467 --> 00:01:06,151
The two new lines are gone, and Git cannot bring them back:

17
00:01:06,151 --> 00:01:12,423
they were never staged or committed, so no object was ever written (lesson 74).

18
00:01:12,423 --> 00:01:16,631
Look at the reflog, the stash list, the object store:

19
00:01:16,631 --> 00:01:17,266
nothing.

20
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:20,411
Prevention is the fix:

21
00:01:20,411 --> 00:01:24,963
anything worth keeping should be at least staged before you experiment.

22
00:01:24,963 --> 00:01:30,669
A staged version is written as an object and survives a restore of the working directory:

23
00:01:32,433 --> 00:01:33,147
Got it.

24
00:01:33,147 --> 00:01:35,898
What do I need to remember?

25
00:01:36,433 --> 00:01:41,473
git restore FILE discards unstaged edits; they cannot be recovered.

26
00:01:42,033 --> 00:01:47,138
It restores the staged version if there is one, otherwise the committed one.

27
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:52,115
Read git diff before restoring; stage what you want to keep.

28
00:01:53,867 --> 00:01:57,146
I keep hearing about unstage files.

29
00:01:57,146 --> 00:01:58,926
What is it, really?

30
00:01:59,467 --> 00:02:04,563
How to take a file back out of the next commit without losing your edit:

31
00:02:04,563 --> 00:02:06,403
git restore --staged FILE.

32
00:02:06,403 --> 00:02:08,456
This undo is completely safe.

33
00:02:10,200 --> 00:02:14,599
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

34
00:02:15,133 --> 00:02:20,351
Two changes staged, but only one belongs in the next commit:

35
00:02:20,351 --> 00:02:22,003
Unstage prices.txt:

36
00:02:22,567 --> 00:02:24,346
Unstage prices.txt:

37
00:02:24,346 --> 00:02:31,367
Column one (staged) is empty for prices.txt, column two (unstaged) shows M:

38
00:02:31,367 --> 00:02:36,891
the edit is still in the file, just not in the next commit.

39
00:02:36,891 --> 00:02:38,951
Commit the menu alone:

40
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:46,300
Column one (staged) is empty for prices.txt, column two (unstaged) shows M:

41
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:51,650
the edit is still in the file, just not in the next commit.

42
00:02:51,650 --> 00:02:53,644
Commit the menu alone:

43
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:57,072
Let me try something.

44
00:02:57,072 --> 00:03:02,484
Unstage the file in a brand-new repository, before the first commit:

45
00:03:03,033 --> 00:03:04,771
could not resolve 'HEAD':

46
00:03:04,771 --> 00:03:11,027
git restore --staged copies the version from HEAD, and a new repository has no commit yet.

47
00:03:11,027 --> 00:03:14,363
git status itself tells you what to use instead:

48
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:17,899
Here is the fix.

49
00:03:19,633 --> 00:03:20,347
Got it.

50
00:03:20,347 --> 00:03:23,098
What do I need to remember?

51
00:03:23,633 --> 00:03:29,058
git restore --staged FILE removes a file from the next commit; the edit stays.

52
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:30,992
It is always safe:

53
00:03:30,992 --> 00:03:34,084
it never touches your working directory.

54
00:03:34,633 --> 00:03:38,793
Before the first commit, use git rm --cached FILE.

55
00:03:40,533 --> 00:03:42,382
How does git reset:

56
00:03:42,382 --> 00:03:44,913
soft, mixed and hard work?

57
00:03:45,467 --> 00:03:50,028
git reset COMMIT moves the current branch back to an earlier commit.

58
00:03:50,028 --> 00:03:55,528
Its three modes decide what happens to the work of the commits you step back over:

59
00:03:55,528 --> 00:03:58,681
keep it staged, keep it unstaged, or delete it.

60
00:04:00,433 --> 00:04:04,833
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

61
00:04:05,367 --> 00:04:05,874
--soft:

62
00:04:05,874 --> 00:04:09,211
undo the last commit, keep its changes staged:

63
00:04:09,211 --> 00:04:14,361
The commit "Price mocha" is gone from the branch; its change is staged,

64
00:04:14,361 --> 00:04:19,076
ready to be committed again (with a better message, for example):

65
00:04:19,633 --> 00:04:24,530
The commit "Price mocha" is gone from the branch; its change is staged,

66
00:04:24,530 --> 00:04:29,013
ready to be committed again (with a better message, for example):

67
00:04:29,567 --> 00:04:31,247
--mixed (the default):

68
00:04:31,247 --> 00:04:33,767
undo the last commit and unstage:

69
00:04:33,767 --> 00:04:34,302
--hard:

70
00:04:34,302 --> 00:04:36,746
undo and throw the changes away:

71
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:40,081
Let me try something.

72
00:04:40,081 --> 00:04:41,586
The classic mistake:

73
00:04:41,586 --> 00:04:45,200
--hard with uncommitted work you wanted to keep.

74
00:04:45,733 --> 00:04:48,049
Two different things were lost:

75
00:04:48,049 --> 00:04:48,199
1.

76
00:04:48,199 --> 00:04:51,337
The commit "Add green tea with its price":

77
00:04:51,337 --> 00:04:56,044
recoverable, it still exists as an object; the reflog knows it.

78
00:04:56,044 --> 00:04:56,193
2.

79
00:04:56,193 --> 00:04:59,107
The uncommitted line "flat white 3.60":

80
00:04:59,107 --> 00:05:02,843
never committed or staged, so it is gone for good.

81
00:05:04,600 --> 00:05:06,381
Bring the commit back:

82
00:05:06,381 --> 00:05:12,534
ORIG_HEAD (or the reflog entry HEAD@{1}) is where main was before the reset:

83
00:05:14,300 --> 00:05:15,013
Got it.

84
00:05:15,013 --> 00:05:17,764
What do I need to remember?

85
00:05:18,300 --> 00:05:25,535
reset moves the branch; --soft keeps changes staged, --mixed unstaged, --hard deletes them.

86
00:05:26,100 --> 00:05:34,275
Commits removed by reset are recoverable (reflog, ORIG_HEAD); uncommitted work removed by --hard is not.

87
00:05:34,833 --> 00:05:38,213
Never reset commits others already have:

88
00:05:38,213 --> 00:05:39,143
use revert.

89
00:05:40,900 --> 00:05:43,120
Why would I need git revert?

90
00:05:43,667 --> 00:05:48,164
git revert COMMIT creates a new commit that undoes an earlier one.

91
00:05:48,164 --> 00:05:55,320
History is not rewritten, so it is the safe way to undo something that is already shared (pushed to main,

92
00:05:55,320 --> 00:05:56,001
deployed).

93
00:05:57,767 --> 00:06:02,166
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

94
00:06:02,700 --> 00:06:07,585
The mocha machine broke, and "Add mocha" is already on the shared main.

95
00:06:07,585 --> 00:06:10,337
Take mocha off the menu by reverting it:

96
00:06:10,337 --> 00:06:13,915
The revert is a normal commit; look at what it does:

97
00:06:14,467 --> 00:06:18,169
The revert is a normal commit; look at what it does:

98
00:06:18,169 --> 00:06:21,658
The commit after it ("Price mocha") is untouched:

99
00:06:21,658 --> 00:06:26,286
revert undoes one commit's change, wherever it is in the history.

100
00:06:28,033 --> 00:06:29,517
Let me try something.

101
00:06:29,517 --> 00:06:33,261
Revert a commit whose lines were changed again later:

102
00:06:33,261 --> 00:06:38,983
"Add green tea" added green tea to menu.txt, and later commits changed that line.

103
00:06:39,533 --> 00:06:42,692
A revert is a merge of the "opposite" change:

104
00:06:42,692 --> 00:06:48,587
if the same lines changed afterwards, it conflicts exactly like a merge (lesson 26).

105
00:06:48,587 --> 00:06:52,588
git status says "You are currently reverting commit ...":

106
00:06:54,333 --> 00:06:57,596
Decide what the file should be (here:

107
00:06:57,596 --> 00:07:01,123
no green tea at all), resolve, continue:

108
00:07:02,867 --> 00:07:03,580
Got it.

109
00:07:03,580 --> 00:07:06,331
What do I need to remember?

110
00:07:06,867 --> 00:07:11,501
revert undoes a commit by adding its opposite; history is kept.

111
00:07:12,067 --> 00:07:18,311
Use revert for anything already pushed; reset only for local, unshared commits.

112
00:07:18,867 --> 00:07:20,825
Reverts can conflict:

113
00:07:20,825 --> 00:07:24,556
resolve, git add, git revert --continue.

114
00:07:26,300 --> 00:07:29,100
What should I know about git reflog?

115
00:07:29,633 --> 00:07:35,672
The reflog is your local diary of every position HEAD and each branch have had:

116
00:07:35,672 --> 00:07:39,035
commits, resets, checkouts, merges, rebases.

117
00:07:39,035 --> 00:07:42,933
Almost anything you "lost" with Git is still in it.

118
00:07:44,667 --> 00:07:49,066
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

119
